## 5. Table and View Types

| Type | Time Travel | Fail-safe | Typical use |
|---|---|---|---|
| Permanent | 0–90 days | 7 days | Curated / production |
| Transient | 0–1 day | None | Raw / staging / rebuildable data |
| Temporary | 0–1 day | None | Session-only scratch |
| External | None | None | Read-only query over S3 files |
| Iceberg | Yes (Snowflake-managed) | None | Open format in your S3 |
| Dynamic | Yes | Yes (unless transient) | Declarative pipelines |
| Hybrid | — | — | Low-latency row lookups with enforced PKs/indexes (Unistore) |

**Cost tip:** raw and staging tables that can be reloaded from S3 should be **TRANSIENT** – no 7-day Fail-safe on data that churns daily. A transient schema/database makes all its tables transient.

Views:
- **Standard view** – stored query, no storage.
- **Secure view** – hides the definition and blocks optimizer data leaks. Required for sharing; can be slightly slower.
- **Materialized view** – stored, auto-maintained results (see section 14).

