## 17. Platform Comparisons (keep balanced)

### Snowflake vs Amazon Redshift
| Area | Snowflake | Redshift |
|---|---|---|
| Storage/compute | Fully separated | Separated on RA3/Serverless |
| Workload isolation | Many warehouses on the same data | WLM queues; data sharing between RA3/Serverless |
| Idle cost | Auto-suspend to $0 compute | Provisioned clusters bill until paused; Serverless bills on use |
| Tuning | No dist/sort keys or VACUUM | DISTKEY/SORTKEY design (largely automated now) |
| Semi-structured | VARIANT + FLATTEN | SUPER + PartiQL |
| Recovery/DevOps | Time Travel, UNDROP, zero-copy clones | Snapshots and restore |
| Cloud | AWS, Azure, GCP | AWS only, deepest AWS integration (Zero-ETL, Lake Formation) |

Redshift still fits AWS-only shops with steady 24×7 workloads (reserved pricing) or heavy existing investment.

### Snowflake vs Databricks
- **Databricks:** Spark-based lakehouse on open Delta Lake files in your storage; strong for ML/AI (MLflow, GPUs), code-heavy transformations, Structured Streaming, unstructured data; more configuration (clusters, OPTIMIZE/VACUUM).
- **Snowflake:** SQL-first, near-zero administration, strong concurrency, governance and data sharing; growing Python/ML (Snowpark, Cortex AI, notebooks) and open formats (Iceberg).
- **Good answer:** "Choose by workload and team skills – SQL analytics/BI and low ops → Snowflake; heavy ML/Spark engineering on open files → Databricks. Many companies run both with Iceberg/Delta as the shared format."

